WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:06.250
Netflix has delivered 13 fresh, steaming hot
cups of Luke Cage and boy, were they worth

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:07.250
the wait!

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:10.990
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and today I'm breaking down the ending of

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:15.720
Luke Cage Season 2 for hidden clues, easter
eggs, and what it all means for Season 3.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.470
Obviously, spoilers ahead for the show so
take care if you're not up-to-date or add

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.770
this to your Watch Later playlist.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:26.040
At the end of season 2, Bushmaster is severely
weakened after Luke thwarted his attempt to

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:31.160
kill Mariah and Tilda tells him he needs rest
and nightshade he can only get in Jamaica.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:35.750
Mariah's in prison charged with multiple murders,
but soon dies at the hands of her own daughter,

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:40.329
which means that Shades' deal to give evidence
on Mariah is no longer valid, so he's arrested

00:00:40.329 --> 00:00:41.679
for his crimes.

00:00:41.679 --> 00:00:45.570
Mariah has set up Luke for a fall by leaving
Harlem's Paradise to him in her will.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:49.640
She thinks the club and the power that comes
with it will act as a siren's song to Harlem's

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.460
hero, corrupting him and everything he stands
for.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:53.879
So, is she right?

00:00:53.879 --> 00:00:58.649
Well, a running theme in Season 2 is the fine
line that separates a hero from a villain,

00:00:58.649 --> 00:01:01.920
whether that's Luke Cage and Bushmaster, or
Luke and Mariah.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.580
And showrunner Cheo Hodari Coker has said
he regards the relationship between Mariah

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:11.229
and Luke as "very similar to Batman and the
Joker" in that "they complete each other.

00:01:11.229 --> 00:01:13.320
One is the reason the other exists."

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.750
And Mariah more or less tells Luke this in
the finale.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:20.460
"You made me find who I really am."

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.430
"In a strange way, we're responsible for each
other."

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:29.259
And there are certainly numerous moments in
the final episode that draw parallels between

00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:32.840
the Stokes family and the path on which Luke
now finds himself.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:35.869
For instance:
We see Luke standing in front of portrait

00:01:35.869 --> 00:01:40.740
of Biggie Smalls wearing a crown, just as
Cottonmouth did last season and Mariah does

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:41.740
this season.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:45.259
And then there are those images of Luke standing
in front of that round window looking down

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:48.969
on Harlem's Paradise, like a king in his castle
surveying his domain.

00:01:48.969 --> 00:01:50.280
everything flows thru here.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.890
"I can watch from above, like a hawk."

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:53.890
"Or a dictator."

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:58.340
We also see Luke's whispered conversations
with his new crew which includes Sugar, one

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:00.210
of the Stokes family's men.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:04.049
And that moment where Misty is shut out of
Luke's new life, after previously working

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:09.289
with him for Harlem, clearly and ominously
echoes the end of The Godfather.

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:13.250
Also shut out by Luke is Claire, who Luke
says should go home when Sugar tells him she's

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:14.640
arrived at the club.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.459
This feels in stark contrast to how Luke defended
Claire against Mariah at the start of the

00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:20.459
season.

00:02:20.459 --> 00:02:23.840
Rosario Dawson's not sure if she'll be in
any more Marvel series after this, so it seems

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.280
like the show is creating an option for her
not to return; while also alienating Luke

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.090
from all his old friends.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.860
We also see Harlem's hero meet with other
crime bosses, like Rosalie Carbone, as Mariah

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.280
did at the auction.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.490
Geographically, Luke is still in Harlem, but
symbolically, he's a long way from his former

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:45.210
home at the barbershop now that DW has kicked
him out for becoming a 'crime boss', a Luke

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:48.040
Corleone, and a Luke Trump, as he calls him.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.280
DW wants the shop to return to the neutral
status it had before Tone murdered Pop there

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:53.569
in season 1.

00:02:53.569 --> 00:02:58.340
According to showrunner Cheo Hodari Choker,
by DW calling Luke "Trump" he's "basically

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:01.740
saying, 'You're deluded if you think the way
you're doing things is going to change things

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:02.940
for the better.'"

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:07.130
And Coker has also compared Luke's path this
season to the Shadowland storyline in the

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:09.590
comics where Daredevil takes over the Hand.

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:13.990
However, there are still signs that Luke certainly
intends for things to be different with him

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:16.100
in charge compared to the Stokes.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:19.740
The portrait of Biggie is taken down from
the wall and replaced with one of Mohammed

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:23.750
Ali, which itself is reminiscent of images
we've seen of Luke throughout the series.

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:28.021
However, the way Cottonmouth's Biggie portrait
is swapped out here reminds me of the end

00:03:28.021 --> 00:03:32.920
of season 1 when Mariah did the same, replacing
it with her Basquiat painting.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:36.910
Luke may momentarily stand in front of Biggie's
crown, but he's adamant that he's not the

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:39.930
king, but rather the sheriff or diplomat of
Harlem.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:45.150
So, maybe Luke will prove Mariah's season
2 schemes against him and Cottonmouth's 'Everybody

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:47.050
wants to be a king' speech wrong.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:52.230
Likewise, when DW accused Luke of taking on
the role of 'a king', Luke replied using a

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:53.760
chess analogy:
"I thought you weren't a king."

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.390
"I'm a rook."

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:56.390
"Wait.

00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:00.390
Don't do this man."

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:04.599
"You don't have to do it like this."

00:04:04.599 --> 00:04:06.129
"Somebody has to."

00:04:06.129 --> 00:04:10.630
That's interesting because this idea of Luke
being a castle wall that protects Harlem picks

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:14.959
up on similar imagery that Mariah used to
describe herself as the 'protector of Harlem'

00:04:14.959 --> 00:04:16.220
to the judge.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:20.270
In Mariah's mind, the Stokes shored up the
wall around the neighbourhood, in other words

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:24.580
by protecting their own criminal empire, they
also protected other people around them.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:28.710
With Mariah out of the game, and crime rampant
again, Luke believes he can try and plug the

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:30.889
holes in the wall himself.

00:04:30.889 --> 00:04:35.100
Luke's new clothes may be more formal than
usual, akin to Cottonmouth and Mariah, but

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:41.419
they still have a golden tinge to them, a
sign that, underneath, this is still the yellow-T-shirt-and-hoodie-wearing

00:04:41.419 --> 00:04:44.110
hero we've met over the last two seasons.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:48.220
As Comanche said earlier this season, 'Even
if you put it in a 3-piece suit, street's

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:49.310
still street.'

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:53.621
So, while the show certainly draws parallels
between Luke and Mariah, the Godfather, and

00:04:53.621 --> 00:04:58.220
the 45th US President, I don't think we'll
see Luke go fully or irredeemably Stokes,

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:00.210
Corleone, or Trump in Season 3.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:05.000
More likely, we'll see him teeter on the edge
and maybe fall over for a while as he struggles

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:09.750
to maintain his integrity, his identity as
a hero, and also his temper, as we saw happen

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:12.220
at Claire's apartment early on in the season.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:15.880
And, of course, it's important to remember
the words of Luke's dad at the very end of

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:20.030
the season 2, which, for me, had vibes of
Uncle Ben's 'with great power comes great

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:21.240
responsibility' speech.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:25.800
"With that kind of power comes its share of
pain."

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.060
"What comes out when that pressure is heaviest?"

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.190
"That's the real magic."

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:36.220
"That's what defines being a man."

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:39.259
"That's what defines being a hero."

00:05:39.259 --> 00:05:42.509
So, expect the pressure on Luke to increase
in Season 3.

00:05:42.509 --> 00:05:47.040
One of those pressure points will likely be
Tilda Johnson, who's rather obviously annoyed

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:52.330
that after all her mother's talk of 'Family
First, Always', Mariah's will only gives her

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:56.229
Cornell's keyboard while leaving Harlem's
Paradise to Luke.

00:05:56.229 --> 00:05:59.849
Tilda's character in the show, where she's
an holistic doctor and Mariah's estranged

00:05:59.849 --> 00:06:03.480
daughter, is rather different from her comic-book
origins where Tilda Johnson, aka Nightshade,

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:09.050
is a genius criminal scientist with a penchant
for turning people into werewolves and using

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:10.850
robots to run Harlem.

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:16.690
Yet, despite those differences, the show includes
numerous hints to TV Tilda's comic-book counterpart,

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:20.620
including her intelligence, her scientific
background, and how she uses Nightshade to

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:22.110
help Bushmaster.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:26.030
And the 'Beso de la Araña', or 'Spider's
Kiss', which Tilda uses to poison her mother

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:30.910
in prison is likely a hat-tip to the Spider-Island
storyline in the comics where Nightshade gains

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:31.919
spider powers.

00:06:31.919 --> 00:06:36.440
And, when she arrives at Harlem's Paradise
after Luke's taken it over, Tilda's look is

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:39.970
a clear nod to the design of her villainous
character in the comics.

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:43.970
All of these comic-book call-backs, as well
as Tilda's anger over the will, and the fact

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:48.580
that she used her herbal knowledge both to
aid Bushmaster and to murder her mother, indicate

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:53.000
that we'll see Tilda return in Season 3, where
she could well be set up as an antagonist

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:54.180
to Luke.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:58.590
Another pressure point on Luke in season 3
will be criminal underworld boss, Rosalie

00:06:58.590 --> 00:06:59.590
Carbone.

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:00.590
Although Carbone only plays a small part in
Season 2, the incredible power she wields

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:01.590
could well be crucial for Season 3.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:04.300
As she tells Luke, she has Russian crime syndicates
on her side as well, so he better tread very

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:06.879
carefully in his future dealings with her.

00:07:06.879 --> 00:07:11.360
While Carbone agrees to wall off Harlem against
the current crime wave, she doesn't see this

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.290
as a long-term thing:
"A lockbox around Harlem?"

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:18.210
"How much would it cost to get a key made?"

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:19.780
"I can't be bought."

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:22.930
"You can't answer that truthfully yet, sweetheart."

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:25.990
"You don't know your price yet."

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:30.150
And at the end of episode 13, there's a potentially
ominous moment when, after the rapper Rakim

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:35.150
says 'you pay with your life in the king's
paradise', the camera cuts to Carbone.

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:39.830
In the comics, Rosalie is part of the Carbone
crime family and falls for Frank Castle, aka

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:44.310
the Punisher, until she realises who he is
after which she puts out a hit on him.

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:48.630
I don't think we'll see Castle turn up in
Luke Cage next season, but what we could see

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:53.169
is Luke taking on a similar role to him, with
Carbone putting the moves on Luke, especially

00:07:53.169 --> 00:07:54.840
given how she ends their meeting.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.370
"Next time, let's do this over coffee."

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:00.060
"I don't like espresso."

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:03.150
"No one's made you a proper cup."

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:08.449
Still, the next time we'll see Carbone will
actually be in Season 3 of Daredevil, so obviously

00:08:08.449 --> 00:08:12.860
whatever happens with her in that show could
well affect her future in this series.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:15.479
Now, what did you think of Season 2 of Luke
Cage?

00:08:15.479 --> 00:08:17.710
And what would you like to see happen next?

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:20.280
Let's continue the conversation in the comments
below.

